\begin{abstract}
We present \textsc{TensorGuard}, a sound static shape verifier for
an explicitly-scoped fragment $\mathcal F$ of PyTorch
\verb|nn.Module| \verb|forward| methods. The verifier parses the
class source, extracts a typed AST graph, and performs symbolic
abstract interpretation over shape tuples (with symbolic batch
and feature dimensions). Out-of-fragment constructs --- opaque
submodules passed in as constructor arguments, helper-built
\texttt{nn.Sequential}s, configuration-attribute indirection
(e.g.\ \texttt{config.n\_embd}), data-dependent control flow ---
trigger \emph{explicit abstention}: the analyzer marks the
relevant subexpression's shape as fully symbolic and continues,
emitting no diagnostic for that subgraph. Soundness is proved
relative to the modeled DSL operators and the abstention policy
(\Cref{thm:soundness}); the verified fragment of the soundness
proof is machine-checked in Lean~4 ($17$ theorems, $0$
\texttt{sorry}). On a real-source torchvision benchmark of $30$
\verb|(module, class)| pairs taken verbatim from the installed
package source, \textsc{TensorGuard} reports $11$ verified-safe,
$19$ abstain-on-opaque-submodule, and \textbf{$0$ false positives}
(\Cref{tab:tv-source}, \texttt{benchmarks/headline\_results.json});
on six self-contained model files fetched from public PyTorch
repositories (nanoGPT, minGPT, MAE, U-Net, labml MHA),
\textsc{TensorGuard}'s shape verdict is $5$ abstain-on-opaque,
$0$ shape-rejected, and $1$ helper-only file skipped
(\Cref{tab:real-repo}). On a curated $14$-model
buggy/safe suite, F$_1=1.00$. We compare against
\verb|torch.fx|+ShapeProp, \verb|FakeTensorMode|, and
\verb|torch.export|, and articulate what \textsc{TensorGuard}
adds: (a) closed-form symbolic shape constraints,
(b) no-execution analysis (works on uninstantiable source),
(c) a scoped \emph{formal} soundness guarantee, and
(d) a first-class abstain verdict.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}

Tensor shape errors are a leading cause of failed PyTorch
training runs. They typically manifest only \emph{after} a
forward pass is launched on the GPU, often deep inside an
autograd-traced subgraph, and some are conditional on the batch
size, the input image resolution, or the data-loader collation,
so they evade unit testing.

\paragraph{Contribution.} We present \textsc{TensorGuard}: a
sound shape verifier for a typed AST fragment $\mathcal F$ of
\texttt{nn.Module} \texttt{forward} methods, with explicit
abstention on out-of-fragment constructs and a Lean-verified
core. Concretely:
\begin{itemize}\itemsep0pt
\item Symbolic abstract interpretation over shape tuples in
  $\mathbb{Z}[\mathcal V]$ (free commutative semiring on dim
  symbols), with a Z3-backed equality check on canonical forms.
\item A typed extraction front-end that recognises in-fragment
  constructors (\texttt{Linear}, \texttt{Conv1d/2d/3d},
  \texttt{BatchNorm}, \texttt{LayerNorm},
  \texttt{Sequential}, etc.) and registers everything else as an
  opaque submodule whose call propagates a fully-symbolic shape.
\item Sound abstention: when a layer's parameters are statically
  unresolvable (e.g.\ \texttt{nn.Linear(config.n\_embd,
    3*config.n\_embd)} with \texttt{config} passed in to
  \texttt{\_\_init\_\_}), or when a \texttt{Sequential} contains
  helper-built sub-layers whose output dim depends on opaque
  computation, the verifier propagates symbolic dims and emits no
  shape diagnostic for that sub-tree. Combined with strict
  reporting on the in-fragment subgraph, this gives zero false
  positives on real torchvision source while catching every
  injected bug in the curated suite.
\item A Lean~4 core ($17$ theorems, no \texttt{sorry}) that
  mechanises the soundness of \texttt{Linear}, \texttt{view},
  \texttt{matmul}/\texttt{bmm}, \texttt{transpose} (involutivity),
  \texttt{permute} (composition), \texttt{Conv2d} (output spatial
  formula, monotonicity), \texttt{ReLU} (shape preservation), and
  binary broadcast (commutativity).
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Verdict vocabulary.} On any \texttt{nn.Module} class,
\textsc{TensorGuard} returns one of three verdicts:
\textsf{verified} (no shape mismatch on any input consistent
with the abstract input shape), \textsf{rejected} (a definite
mismatch with witness assignment), or \textsf{abstain} (the trace
contains opaque subexpressions; no soundness claim is made about
their internals). \textsf{abstain} is a first-class outcome and
is reported in every benchmark table.

\section{Background and problem statement}\label{sec:background}

PyTorch shape semantics are dynamic: the shape of a tensor is
determined at runtime by the values of integer dimension
parameters. Operators like \texttt{torch.matmul},
\texttt{nn.Linear}, \texttt{view}, \texttt{cat}, and broadcast
\texttt{+} all impose preconditions on these dimensions; when a
precondition fails, PyTorch raises a \texttt{RuntimeError} on the
forward pass.

\textsc{TensorGuard} verifies that, for the given abstract input
shape, every operator inside the forward method's in-fragment
subtree has its precondition discharged symbolically. The
``in-fragment subtree'' is precisely the set of AST nodes that
the extractor types as a known DSL operator
(\Cref{app:ops-extended}); everything else is opaque.

The closest peer tools are
\texttt{torch.fx.symbolic\_trace}+\texttt{ShapeProp},
\texttt{FakeTensorMode}, and \texttt{torch.export}. All three
\emph{execute} the model on a meta or fake input; all three
require the model to be instantiable (i.e., the constructor must
not raise, all dependent modules must import). All three are
\emph{value-level}: they propagate concrete (or fake-concrete)
shape integers, not symbolic constraints over them. They have no
notion of an unannotated opaque call besides ``forward execution
fails''. \Cref{tab:tools} summarises the comparison.

