\section{Symbolic shape abstract interpretation}\label{sec:abstract}

Let $\mathcal V$ be a countable set of \emph{dimension symbols}
(typically $\{B,\ N,\ H,\ W,\ \dots\}$), and let
$\mathcal D = \mathbb Z_{>0}\cup \mathcal V$ be the abstract
dimension domain. An \emph{abstract shape} is a tuple
$s\in\mathcal D^*$. The set of abstract shapes is partially
ordered by \emph{instantiation refinement}: $s\sqsubseteq s'$ iff
there exists a substitution $\sigma\colon\mathcal V\to
\mathcal D$ with $\sigma(s) = s'$. The bottom element is the
all-symbolic shape of unknown rank (used as the abstain
representative).

A DSL operator $f$ has a transfer function
$\widetilde f \colon \mathcal D^{*\,k}\to \mathcal D^*$
together with a precondition predicate
$\mathrm{pre}_f \colon \mathcal D^{*\,k}\to \{T, F, ?\}$. The
analyzer evaluates the forward method by topological traversal
(\Cref{alg:verify}); at each operator it asks $\mathrm{pre}_f$
on the current abstract operand shapes and either
(i) emits \textsf{rejected} with witness if $\mathrm{pre}_f$
returns $F$,
(ii) propagates $\widetilde f$ if $\mathrm{pre}_f$ returns $T$,
or (iii) propagates a fresh symbolic shape and continues if
$\mathrm{pre}_f$ returns $?$ (abstention). Equality of two
symbolic dimensions is decided by canonical-form normalisation
in $\mathbb Z[\mathcal V]$: factor symbolic terms, fold integer
factors, sort lexicographically; equality holds iff the
canonical forms coincide. This is sound and complete on the
free commutative semiring fragment.

\paragraph{Out-of-fragment constructs.} Three categories trigger
abstention:
(\textbf{O1}) opaque submodules: \texttt{self.X = features} where
\texttt{features} is an \texttt{\_\_init\_\_} parameter, or
\texttt{self.X = make\_layers(cfg)} where \texttt{make\_layers}
is a non-DSL helper;
(\textbf{O2}) unresolved layer parameters:
\texttt{nn.Linear(config.n\_embd, ...)} where \texttt{config} is
an opaque object;
(\textbf{O3}) data-dependent control flow inside forward
(\texttt{if x.size(-1) > 0: ...}). In all three, the analyzer
substitutes a fresh symbolic shape for the affected sub-tree;
downstream symbolic equalities then either decide concretely (if
the abstention does not propagate to a precondition) or in turn
abstain.

\begin{algorithm}[t]
\caption{\textsc{TensorGuard} verification of an
\texttt{nn.Module}}\label{alg:verify}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require model $M$, abstract input shape $s_0$
\State $\mathit{state}\gets\{\mathit{out}_0\mapsto s_0\}$
\State $\mathit{traced}\gets\textsc{Trace}(M)$
        \Comment{static AST/graph trace, no execution}
\For{each op $f$ in topological order with inputs $u_1,\ldots,u_k$}
   \State $s_1,\ldots,s_k\gets\mathit{state}[u_1],\ldots,\mathit{state}[u_k]$
   \If{$\mathrm{pre}_f(s_1,\ldots,s_k) = F$}
       \State \Return \textsf{rejected}, witness$=(f,s_1,\ldots,s_k)$
   \ElsIf{$\mathrm{pre}_f(s_1,\ldots,s_k) = ?$ \textbf{ or }
          any $s_i$ is fully symbolic from O1--O3}
       \State $\mathit{state}[\mathit{out}_f]\gets$ fresh symbolic
                shape of rank $\mathrm{rank}(\widetilde f)$
   \Else
       \State $\mathit{state}[\mathit{out}_f]\gets\widetilde f(s_1,\ldots,s_k)$
   \EndIf
\EndFor
\If{the trace contained any abstention} \Return \textsf{abstain}
\Else\ \Return \textsf{verified}
\EndIf
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{theorem}[Soundness, scoped]\label{thm:soundness}
Let $M$ be an \texttt{nn.Module} class with forward method
$\mathrm{fwd}_M$. Suppose every operator on the in-fragment
sub-tree of $\mathrm{fwd}_M$ is one of the modeled DSL operators
listed in \Cref{app:ops-extended}, and every \texttt{Conv2d}
output spatial dim and \texttt{view(-1, ...)} entry satisfies the
divisibility hypotheses of \Cref{app:floor-divisibility}. If
\textsc{TensorGuard} returns \textsf{verified} on input shape
$s_0$, then for every concrete instantiation
$\sigma\colon\mathcal V\to\mathbb Z_{>0}$ with
$\sigma(s_0)$ a valid PyTorch shape, the forward method
$\mathrm{fwd}_M$ runs to completion without any
\texttt{RuntimeError} attributable to a shape mismatch on the
in-fragment sub-tree.
\end{theorem}

The proof is by induction on the number of trace steps;
\Cref{sec:soundness-proof} gives the full argument and
\Cref{sec:lean-theorems} lists the Lean-verified instances. The
core fragment ($17$ theorems, no \texttt{sorry}) covers
\texttt{Linear}, \texttt{view} (with both divisibility lemmas),
\texttt{matmul}/\texttt{bmm}, \texttt{transpose}, \texttt{permute}
(composition), \texttt{Conv2d} (output spatial monotonicity),
\texttt{ReLU} (shape preservation), and binary broadcast
(commutativity). The Lean development requires no
\texttt{mathlib}; \verb|lake build| terminates in $<\!10$\,s.

